# UIBC golden fixtures: byte-deterministic cross-platform (archive SS32).
#
# Root cause of the #177 S4 failure: evidence files were written in Python
# text mode without `newline="\n"`, so on Windows they gained CRLF while the
# stored git blob was LF. The manifest recorded the CRLF content hash, so a
# Linux/macOS (LF) checkout failed S4 while Windows (CRLF) passed -- the same
# signature package verified differently per platform.
#
# Fix (two layers):
#   1. Generators/library now write all fixture content with newline="\n" (LF).
#   2. This attribute forces LF on BOTH store and checkout for every file under
#      fixtures/, so content hashes are identical on every platform.
fixtures/** text eol=lf

# Exception, and it is deliberate: `anchored_head.json` is the file an external RFC 3161
# token was taken over. The token fixes its bytes (CRLF, digest 4ad23d91…), so any
# normalisation changes the digest and makes the token look like it dates something else.
# Marked `-text` the bytes travel unchanged, which is what a byte-level anchor requires.
# Everything else under fixtures/ stays LF per the rule above.
tests/fixtures/anchor/anchored_head.json -text
tests/fixtures/anchor/*.tsr -text
tests/fixtures/anchor/*.ots -text
tests/fixtures/anchor/*.json -text
tests/fixtures/anchor/*.pem -text

# The published corpus is rebuilt byte for byte (`tests/test_oracle_reproducible.py`),
# so its JSON travels LF on store and on checkout, on every platform. The exception
# is the same kind as above: `anchored_head.json` under s16 is the file a real
# RFC 3161 token was taken over, so its bytes are fixed by the token, not by us.
conformance/oracle/**/*.json text eol=lf
conformance/oracle/cases/oracle-s16-token-byte/timestamps/anchored_head.json -text

# Vendored specification sources are hashed in `docs/spec-sources/MANIFEST.json` and
# `tests/test_sources.py` compares those digests with the bytes on disk. Same failure
# mode as #177, different directory: on a default Windows checkout (`core.autocrlf=true`)
# every one of these files gains CRLF and the recorded digest stops matching. Measured
# 2026-11-22 on a plain Windows checkout: 1,294 of 1,710 tracked files were CRLF in the
# worktree while their blobs were LF, and 31 test ids failed for that reason alone -- none
# of them arithmetic. Pinning the text ones to LF on store and checkout removes the class.
docs/spec-sources/** text eol=lf
tests/fixtures/** text eol=lf

# Re-assert the byte-fixed exceptions after the wildcard above: these files are the
# preimages of real RFC 3161 tokens, so their bytes are fixed by someone else.
tests/fixtures/anchor/anchored_head.json -text
tests/fixtures/anchor/*.tsr -text
tests/fixtures/anchor/*.ots -text

# Final override: with 	ext unset git ignores ol, but the wildcard above still
# assigns eol=lf to these. Unset both so nothing normalises the token preimages.
tests/fixtures/anchor/** -text -eol